    "case_summary": "A 67-year-old male presented with ten days of diarrhea, experiencing yellow watery stool about ten times per day, accompanied by fatigue, poor appetite and abdominal distention. Medical history includes decompensated cirrhosis caused by Hepatitis B complicated with alcoholism for 5 years. Patient had been taking entecavir orally with negative HBV-DNA loading.\n\nPatient was alert and oriented with uncomfortable appearance. Vital signs: temperature 38.6°C, blood pressure 124/75 mmHg, pulse rate 95 beats/minute, respiratory rate 20 breaths/minute, oxygen saturation 98%. Skin and sclera showed obvious jaundice. Cardiopulmonary examination revealed no abnormal findings. Abdomen was distended with high tension. Edema present in both lower limbs.\n\nLaboratory Tests:\n- Elevated C-reactive protein (59.32 mg/L)\n- Procalcitonin (0.63 ng/ml)\n- Anemia (hemoglobin 11.6 g/dL)\n- WBC count 793/mm3 with 80.6% neutrophils\n- Albumin 24.4 g/L\n- Total bilirubin 55.6 umol/L\n- Direct bilirubin 25.6 umol/L\n- Prolonged prothrombin time (18.3s)\n- Active partial thromboplastin time (44.7s)\n- Decreased prothrombin activity (54%)\n- INR 1.51\n- D-dimer 7004.4 ng/ml\n\nImaging Studies:\n- Chest CT: Title: Chest CT findings\n  Description: Scattered pneumonia of both sides and small amount of bilateral pleural effusion\n\n- Abdominal CT: Title: Contrast-enhanced CT abdomen and pelvis\n  Description: Liver cirrhosis, splenomegaly, massive ascites. Low-density cystic mass with peripheral rim and septal enhancement in right posterior perinephric space, measuring 9.2 cm × 4.8 cm in axial sections and 15.8 cm in coronal sections. Mass involved right psoas major muscle and extended to iliac fossa.\n\nMicrobiological Studies:\n- Pus culture from abscess positive for Klebsiella pneumoniae ssp pneumoniae\n- Blood, stool, and ascites cultures were negative",
